571 0 obj
<<
/Title (This study presents a reproduction of the machine learning methodology reported in Paper 3, "An)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 407 0 R
/Next 712 0 R
>>
endobj
572 0 obj
<<
/Title (empirical investigation of data duplication and train-test contamination. The reported methodology was reproduced using the complete 1,025-record heart disease dataset. The reproduction produced unusually high predictive performance, although the exact numerical results differed from those reported in the original paper. Random Forest, XGBoost, and the proposed Stacking Ensemble achieved 100.00% accuracy and an AUC of 1.0000, compared with the published Stacking Ensemble performance of 98.53% accuracy and 0.9880 AUC. To investigate the stability and validity of these results, an independent exact-record deduplication analysis was subsequently performed. The original dataset contained 723 duplicate observations, leaving 302 unique observations after exact-record deduplication. Re-evaluation on this deduplicated cohort substantially changed the observed model performance. The Stacking Ensemble decreased to 75.41% accuracy and 0.8626 AUC, while Logistic Regression achieved the highest accuracy of 80.33%. Gaussian Naive Bayes achieved the highest AUC of 0.8842. An additional duplicate index-overlap analysis found that 202 out of 205 test observations in the original 1,025-record experiment had exact duplicate counterparts in the training partition, corresponding to 98.54% of the test set. This provides direct empirical evidence of train-test contamination caused by duplicate observations and offers a strong methodological explanation for the unusually optimistic performance observed on the full dataset. The findings demonstrate the importance of duplicate auditing, appropriate data partitioning, and independent validation when evaluating machine learning models for medical prediction tasks.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 407 0 R
/Prev 712 0 R
>>
endobj
